face the nation moderator and senior  foreign affairs correspondent Margaret  Brennan is here with CBS News senior  global affairs contributor Ian Bremmer  he is the president of the Eurasia group  good morning to you both President Trump  said today he didn't give up anything  and yet he said he would suspend these  joint military exercises with the South  Koreans this morning the South Koreans  responded by saying we're trying to  figure out exactly what Trump meant is  this a big concession it is it's  tremendous to have the South Koreans say  this because they are our partners our  allies in those military exercises the  Pentagon has also not said is this a  halt a suspension an end how long does  this last I mean there are so many  details that we don't know in terms of  this nebulous security guarantee that's  mentioned here in this statement does it  go beyond that the president has at  least three times been asked by  reporters since that press conference  what else did you promise in terms of  security guarantees and he will not go  into it it's gonna be up to Mike Pompeo  was flying at China and South Korea to  work out the details more details in the  United States has made agreements with  North Korea in the past 1994 2005 2012  the North Koreans have this great  strategy they string us along they ring  out new economic stuff and then it's  just talk and they could continue their  nuclear weapons program why should we  believe them now we shouldn't believe  them and I mean it's a little surprising  to see Trump say I trust the guy it's  more of a stronger formulation than Bush  looking into Putin's eyes and saying I  saw a soul might live to regret that but  Kim jong-un is a different leader a  younger leader focusing more on the  economic engagement and the openness  he's done over the course of the past  few months with the South Koreans summit  twice now with the South Korean  president twice with Xi Jinping once  with the Russian for minister lavrov  going to Pyongyang and now Trump that's  the story here's that the North Koreans  are opening up and Trump as a piece of  it let's keep in mind what we now have  is a freeze for freeze it is the North  Koreans are freezing their ICBM and  nuclear tests and the Americans are  freezing our military exercises with the  South Koreans that is exactly the  formation of the Chinese have asked for  over the course of the past year and  we've said absolutely not margit let me  ask you about this key question of  denuclearization in the in the agreement  as I read it it is just a react for  of it point number three a reaffirmation  of a previous promise that the North  Koreans have made to work toward work  towards the crucial fact working towards  as opposed to agreeing to so put that in  context for us what exactly was agreed  to here that the North Koreans have not  already previously agreed to on nukes  well we don't know what we know from the  president said is that they've started  to break apart some of their missiles  that's part of the irreversible step he  wanted to see Secretary of State Mike  Pompeo is flying to these Asian capitals  to explain what happened in the room but  he's gonna have to brief Congress  they're gonna be a lot of questions  about exactly what that means I think  one of the interesting things is if you  take a step back it took two years of  direct talks with Iran to get them to  [ __ ] about 97% of their enriched uranium  break up thousands of their centrifuges  and hold their program and they didn't  have anything like the arsenal that we  know and believe North Korea has and  Secretary of State Mike Pompeo as a  congressman raised holy hell about that  deal so he has set for himself and a  tremendous benchmark as a president  Trump in terms of what they now need to  get in terms of details access to  military sites actual American  inspectors on the ground we know that's  what America wants we don't know if  that's what they got agreed to in this  room we know that's what John Bolton has  said he wants American inspectors to be  taking the lead to break apart these  weapons so that it doesn't happen again  that they don't cheat like they have in  the past so what stood out to both of  you in this agreement that we've reached  today and is there a win for both sides  look I think what the biggest win is  that six months ago we were talking  about the possibility of military  preemption against North Korea  it's almost inconceivable now that there  is a short-term risk of military  conflict that's a win for the North  Korean and the South Korean people first  among all but also Trump has gotten this  historic summit he did get the Chinese  to squeeze the North Koreans harder on  sanctions the North Koreans came to the  table before Trump started that the only  foreign leader Kim jong-un did mammoth  was Dennis Rodman right so let's there  is something historic that here is it is  important but as Margaret suggested it's  a very high bar to go from here to  complete verifiable irreversible  denuclearization and the North Koreans  didn't actually take any concrete step  towards that yesterday what  that the president dangled today was the  removing of some 30,000 US troops that  have been in the Korean Peninsula  I lived there it has a kid you know miss  is a long time that we've had a  commitment to that area if we were to  remove troops from the Korean Peninsula  it doesn't just affect the Korean  Peninsula affects our entire military  strategic posture in Asia doesn't it  isn't that really what China wants and  this is exactly not only what Ian  highlighted there in terms of the freeze  first freeze language that China wanted  this is what China wants North Korea  isn't the only flashpoint in Asia as you  point out there are a lot of concerns  about clashes in South China Sea also a  big winner here at South Korea's moon  jae-in because he was looking  desperately for an off-ramp he was very  concerned about this ratcheting up of  military tensions and he delivered he  pulled off this coup of getting these  South Korean intelligence officers into  the oval office back in March to brief  the president the president said today  and this was interesting I've had a  relationship with Kim jong-un for about  90 days that takes you back to March he  hasn't said if he's spoken to him before  talked on the phone is that what you're  saying it seems to me that's what he may  be dancing around there we've had a  relationship for 90 days I've been to 90  days what about when he was saying I  trust him what about the personal  chemistry between the two I mean they  both seem to enjoy this photo op the the  way that they touched each other the way  that they smiled at each other you know  this goes from really very personal  name-calling  that wasn't so long ago look I mean you  remember the way the Kim show that the  North Korean leader his father was  portrayed in Team America I'm so lonely  I mean you know both Trump and Kim are  leaders that clearly really want some  legitimization right they want to be  treated well but I would just say the  most important takeaway here long-term  is that the United States is probably  going to be a much more marginal player  at the end of the day in this region and  you know in the same way that Syria kind  of defined the Obama administration he  ultimately wasn't that major play in the  ground  that's kind of what we're seeing here  China's the big winner much more to talk  about Ian Brown there and Mark Brennan  thank you so much

